#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Run an interactive Claude Code agent in tmux and recover transient failures.

Mirror of tools/agent-supervisor (the Codex supervisor) for the Claude Code
backend. The tmux/pty plumbing, the backoff ladders, the rotation budget, the
run deadline and the state.json contract an external collector can poll are
the same. What differs is how the agent is observed and steered:

- The session id is chosen up front (``claude --session-id``), so the transcript
  is found by name under ``<claude-home>/projects/*/<id>.jsonl`` instead of
  discovered by content; ``claude --resume <id>`` reopens and appends to it.
- A turn ends with an ``assistant`` line whose ``stop_reason`` is ``end_turn``;
  an API failure is an ``assistant`` line flagged ``isApiErrorMessage`` with the
  error code in ``error``. Subagent traffic is ``isSidechain`` and ignored.
- Claude Code has no goal store: an agent that gives up says so in its final
  message (GAVE_UP_RE), an agent that stops to ask a question is nudged on in
  the same session (``awaiting_input``), and a completed turn is followed by the
  supervisor stopping the idle TUI after COMPLETE_GRACE_SECONDS so the run
  reaches a terminal ``exited`` status on its own.
- That last stop must also account for *background subagents*. Each writes to
  ``<transcript-stem>/subagents/agent-<id>.{jsonl,meta.json}``, a sibling tree
  of the session transcript, and an orchestrator that spawns a batch ends its
  turn to wait for them -- so the file this supervisor tails goes silent while
  the run is at its busiest. Stopping on the parent's quiet alone kills the
  batch and records the truncated run as a clean success. The batch is counted
  from the transcript itself: the ``Agent`` tool call returns *immediately* for
  a background agent (a ``tool_result`` reading "Async agent launched"), and the
  agent reports back later as a ``<task-notification>`` naming the launching
  ``tool_use`` id -- as a user message, or only as a ``queue-operation`` line
  when the orchestrator was mid-turn. Only the notification means the agent
  is done, and only until the orchestrator resumes it: a ``SendMessage`` to a
  reported agent rewrites its meta.json, and that agent is outstanding again.
- Every transcript this supervisor discovers is listed in ``state.json`` under
  ``transcripts`` so an external collector can archive them.
  They live outside the run dir and are the only record of what a batch did.
"""

